<%# locals: (faqs:) -%>
<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best time for skiing in the Alps is January through March, with high-altitude resorts like Val d'Isère, Tignes, and Zermatt often skiing well earlier. The Alps differ from North America in scale (huge linked ski areas between villages), in how trails are rated (blue, red, black), and in safety: off-piste terrain is usually not patrolled or avalanche-controlled. Fly into Geneva for most of France and western Switzerland, Zurich for eastern Switzerland, Innsbruck for Austria, and Venice or Innsbruck for the Dolomites.</p></div>

<h2>How the Alps differ from North American skiing</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scale:</strong> many Alpine ski areas link several villages and even countries with one lift network, so you can ski all day without repeating a run.</li>
  <li><strong>Villages, not base areas:</strong> resorts are often historic towns with shops, restaurants, and train stations, rather than purpose-built base lodges.</li>
  <li><strong>Mountain huts:</strong> sit-down lunches at on-mountain restaurants are part of the culture. Expect table service, local food, and a slower midday pace. See <%= link_to "what is après-ski", guide_path("what-is-apres-ski") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Lift tickets:</strong> walk-up day tickets are often less expensive than at major North American resorts, and multi-day area passes are common. Prices vary widely by resort and season.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Piste vs off-piste</h3>
<p>In the Alps, marked and groomed runs are called pistes, and they're patrolled. Terrain off the marked pistes, even when it's right beside a lift, is generally not patrolled or controlled for avalanches. That's a big change from North America, where most in-bounds terrain is managed. If you leave the piste, you're in avalanche terrain: check the local avalanche bulletin, carry a beacon, probe, and shovel, and hire a mountain guide. Read <%= link_to "avalanche safety gear explained", guide_path("avalanche-safety-gear") %>.</p>

