<h3>France</h3>
<p>Chamonix sits below Mont Blanc and is famous for steep, high-alpine terrain best explored with a guide. Val d'Isère and Tignes share a large, high ski area with a long season. Courchevel is part of Les 3 Vallées, one of the largest linked ski areas in the world. See the <%= link_to "France snow report", region_path("france") %>.</p>
<h3>Switzerland</h3>
<p>Zermatt skis beneath the Matterhorn, with high terrain and lifts that connect toward Italy. Verbier, in the 4 Vallées, is known for its off-piste. St. Moritz in the Engadin valley is a classic, sunny winter resort town. See the <%= link_to "Switzerland snow report", region_path("switzerland") %>.</p>
<h3>Austria</h3>
<p>St. Anton is the heart of Ski Arlberg and the lively après scene. Kitzbühel is a historic town known for the Hahnenkamm race. Ischgl links with Samnaun across the Swiss border. See the <%= link_to "Austria snow report", region_path("austria") %>.</p>
<h3>Italy</h3>
<p>Cortina d'Ampezzo and Val Gardena are in the Dolomites, where one Dolomiti Superski pass covers many valleys and the scenery is dramatic. Cortina co-hosted the 2026 Winter Olympics. See the <%= link_to "Italy snow report", region_path("italy") %>.</p>

<h2>Epic and Ikon Pass in the Alps</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ikon:</strong> Chamonix, Zermatt, St. Moritz, Kitzbühel, and Ischgl each have 7 days on the Ikon Pass and 5 on the Ikon Base Pass. Cortina d'Ampezzo and Val Gardena are covered through Dolomiti Superski, with 7 days (5 on Base) shared across all Dolomiti Superski areas. Chamonix also has 2 days on the Mountain Collective.</li>
  <li><strong>Epic:</strong> Courchevel has 7 consecutive days at Les 3 Vallées, and Verbier has 5 consecutive days at Verbier 4 Vallées. St. Anton has 3 consecutive days at Ski Arlberg, only with a stay booked directly at a participating Arlberg lodging partner.</li>
  <li><strong>Neither:</strong> Val d'Isère and Tignes sell their own lift passes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pass rules change each season; confirm on the pass website before you book. Compare on <%= link_to "Ikon vs Epic", ikon_vs_epic_path %>.</p>

<h2>When to go</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>December:</strong> high resorts and glacier areas usually open first. Lower villages can be thin before Christmas.</li>
  <li><strong>January:</strong> often cold with good snow and fewer crowds after New Year.</li>
  <li><strong>February:</strong> European school holidays make it the busiest month, so book early.</li>
  <li><strong>March:</strong> deep bases, longer days, and sunshine; a favorite month at high resorts.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each resort page has its own monthly snow pattern, such as <%= link_to "best time to ski Chamonix", best_time_resort_path("chamonix") %> and <%= link_to "best time to ski St. Anton", best_time_resort_path("st-anton") %>.</p>

<h2>Getting there</h2>
<p>Geneva (GVA) serves most French resorts plus Zermatt and Verbier. Zurich (ZRH) serves St. Moritz and eastern Switzerland. Innsbruck (INN) serves the Tyrol and is the closest major airport to Val Gardena; Venice (VCE) is the usual gateway to Cortina. Trains and transfer shuttles reach most resorts, and some car-free villages like Zermatt are reached only by train. See <%= link_to "how to plan a ski trip", guide_path("how-to-plan-a-ski-trip") %>.</p>
<p>All 12 resorts are ranked by this week's forecast on the <%= link_to "Alps snow report", region_path("the-alps") %>.</p>

